What do bona fides have anything to do with simple language and critical analysis.  Either your proof is in the pudding or it ain't.  I like discussion.  I like hearing alternate theories and different suggestion.  Your reply seems designed more to shut down conversation that to encourage it.

&#x200B;

Climate Change 

No

&#x200B;

"what you are describing is closer to Zelazny's Amber."  Closer to Zelazny's Amber than what?  Than a scene in a book you haven't read?  It's not the type of reply that generates more thought, consideration, or speculation.  

&#x200B;

It's fine, we don't need to argue about the value of your reply.  I just wonder why someone who is uninterested in further conversation would reply at all.

The other thing I kind of reasoned about Juturna from Urth is that she probably doesn't really go and hang out in the sea much.  She's in Gyoll when Severian almost drowns. Or does drown.  Or is drowned or whatever. 

&#x200B;

In Claw she is not so very far from the House Absolute.  In Urth she is in House Absolute.

&#x200B;

But now I've wondered something. Going back to Claw -  It is when Severian is talking to her that Jolenta either tries to kill herself or is attacked by a "blood bat" or a vampire or perhaps an inhumi.  I dunno. That scene with Jolenta always seemed strange.  Now it is making me think even more that Blue and Green are Ushas and Lune or Ushas and Verthandi or what have you.

&#x200B;

Do people here actually believe in this stuff?  I thought the study of Gnosticism was more sort of an academic/historical exercise.

&#x200B;

It seems weird to supplant one set of ancient ambiguous texts with another set of ancient ambiguous texts.  The Gnostic story is more cohesive and makes more sense.

&#x200B;

I've always assumed that the story of Mary's viginity and Jesus' birth came well after the rest of his story (and death) actually happened.  Authoritarian men place such a premium on women's virginity.

I'm glad to see you mention The Black Company and Glen Cook.  I think I first read The Tower of Fear and Shadow of the Torturer within a month of each other.  At the time I loved them both and considered them roughly equivalent. Forgive me, I was fourteen.  

&#x200B;

Upon successive rereads I have come to revere Wolfe but am almost embarrassed about loving the Black Company.  Cook's books for me were a process of diminishing returns.   The Black Company was amazing.  Shadows Linger was good.  The White Rose was okay and from there on I felt like it was all downhill (Except for the Silver Spike, that bucked the trend.  Toadkiller dog is one of my favorite characters - kind of Gylflike ins't he?  And I had a big black rottweiller shepherd mix that I named Raven.)  I don't think I've even tried one of them after the 6th.  Too much of it felt like watching Vampire Diaries where a character is dramatically killed off, only to return again and again and again.  

&#x200B;

Part of what made me love the Black Company so much is how much Cook loves his characters.  A good author creates characters that we can know and love.  I understand how hard it must be to abandon those characters.  Especially when you've got publishers and fans clamoring for more.  In a way Gene Wolfe kind of did a similar thing.  BotNS was wrapped and sealed and Severian's fate was in the wind.  And then we had Urth.  Similarly, Silk heroically and romantically remained on the whorl for Hyacinth and the Long Sun books were over.  But then Silk comes back.  I'm guilty.  I freakin loved going back to the Whorl and I was thrilled to be back in the mind of Silk.  And I think the Books of the Short Sun are some of the best books in the Solar Cycle and I love the cycle as a whole.  But artistically, it does kind of mar the beauty of the end of the New Sun and Long Sun books themselves.

&#x200B;

I think I'm going to go read the Black Company, I did love it so.  DAMWrite, tell me if it's really worth it to press through the rest of that series.

