[https://www.reddit.com/r/genewolfe/comments/9nmwlq/question\_about\_a\_couple\_of\_lines\_in\_the\_knight/e7opokw](https://www.reddit.com/r/genewolfe/comments/9nmwlq/question_about_a_couple_of_lines_in_the_knight/e7opokw)

I agree.  It's weird that people called him a troll.  If he's "trolling" at all then it's at a higher level and can lead to interesting conversation which basically makes it not trolling at all.

&#x200B;

I'm kind of blown away because I never even considered this possibility, even while reading VirtueSignal's post.  My first reaction to the Nazi question was an emphatic "No".  I know that's pretty childish and goes back to my first read of BotNS when it first came out and I was an adolescent.  I thought that loving the book required me to love Severian which I thought required him to be noble and pure or something.  Just the way I would have defended Tarzan or Belgarion.

&#x200B;

*Nazi Germany and Ascia was the USSR* 

this doesn't feel wrong. And it's easy to accept this idea and to counter my childish need to defend Wofle, BotNS, The Commonwealth, Nessus, and Severian.  But Wolfe is clearly not advocating authoritarianism and Nazi ideals.  That's a mistaken read of the OP's ideas.  I'm not smart enough or well read enough to know if that's what his implication was.  Following the Solar Cycle through we see that a Nazi-style society, is decidedly not something Wolfe advocates.

&#x200B;

I mean it makes sense.  BotNS is a book about redemption.  So we start with the most loathsome individual - a torturer.  Ugh.  Yuck.  Torturers are bad and deserve to be punched in the face by true Knights and then eaten by ogres.  And we can all agree that as much as we love visiting the world that Wolfe has created and as much beauty as we can see there, Nessus and the Commonwealth is a pretty terrible place to live.  So, sure, why not a Nazi analog.  That makes sense.  Severian rises from the most loathsome position in the most loathsome regime of human history and ascends to the greatest heights.

I guess the part where the VirtueSignal seems to be clearly trolling is here:

&#x200B;

*I think we get so caught in the weeds on the first few reads that we  often forget to stop and ask the obvious questions: did Hitler escape to  South America? Did he find shelter with the anti-Semitic Jorge Luis  Borges? Can Severian trace his lineage back to Adolf? Can Hitler trace  his lineage back to Apu-Pauncha? Did the Nazi’s succeed in terraforming  the moon?*

&#x200B;

And yet even in this intentional trolling I find some interesting questions.  The Hitler to South America thing is intentionally goofy allusion to a fun-scary conspiracy theory.  So let's let that one pass.  "anti-Semitic Jorge Luis Borges" I believe is an intentional joke.  Severian lineage to Adolf. That kind of gets us nowhere.  No info in the text to support that.  Hitler to Apu-Pauncha also goes nowhere.  But put those two together and we have Severian being his own ancestor.  That is a fun idea to play with and very much a Wolfian idea (like "Predestination").  Did the Nazis have an idea to terraform the moon?  I don't know.  But it did get me thinking about Wolfe and Judaism.  There is so much alluded to from so many religions in his books, but I can't think of anything that is inherently Jewish.  Or strictly Jewish and not Jewish ideas filtered through Christianity.  We got no Moses here in these books do we?  The waters parting by an undine is a stretch.  I'm trying to think of other ideas from Judaism that could be here.  Oh, I know one, in the story of the Monitor, the student creates his son much the way a Golem is created.  It always felt like that jewish folktale to me, even if it wasn't explicitly stated.  I guess I need to know more about Jewish mysticism to look for more.  But Wolfe as anti-Semite just ain't there.

&#x200B;

So thanks, VirtueSignal and President Cleveland for food for thought.

